{
  "id": "clock-behind-remint",
  "title": "Reviews made with a reset clock are re-minted, not lost",
  "covers": [
    "clock: a review refused with clock_behind is re-minted with a corrected reviewed_at (SYNC.md 10.4)",
    "replay: the re-minted reviews keep their spacing, so FSRS elapsed times are right"
  ],
  "narrative": [
    "A creates a note and its card at T and syncs. Then A's real-time clock resets to 2000-01-01; offline, A reviews the card three times over four days (1 h, 1 d and 3 d after the reset).",
    "A syncs with the clock still wrong: the three reviews are refused with clock_behind (reviewed_at before 2020). A records remint_offset = server_time - pt of that response (about 25 years).",
    "The user fixes the clock. A's next response shows it correct, and A runs Re-mint: one transaction discards the three rejected rows, mints three new review.logged ops (new ids, new HLCs, original order) with reviewed_at' = reviewed_at + delta, and sets rebuild_pending.",
    "delta = remint_offset here, because the shifted batch lies between the card's creation and now; the reviews keep their 1 d and 3 d spacing. The re-minted ops are accepted (seq 3-5); A rebuilds from the log; every replica holds the re-minted rows only.",
    "`remint` names the originals (in `rejected`) and the re-minted ops (in `log`); tools/validate.py recomputes the shift from them."
  ],
